{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>For there is no divination in Jacob</b> – They have no need for divination and sorcery, such that one should declare to Jacob and Israel what El has wrought and what His decrees are on high. They do not divine or practice sorcery; rather, it is declared to them through the mouth of prophets what the decree of the Place is, or the Urim and Tummim make it known to them — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 23:23). And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that because he was a sorcerer and Balak sent to him, \"For I know that he whom you bless is blessed, and he whom you curse is cursed\" (Numbers 22:6), and he sent him sorceries, therefore he said to him: There is no divination in Jacob to do them harm or good, and no sorcery in Israel that harms or benefits, for at every time it is declared to Jacob and Israel what El has wrought among them — for from the mouth of the Most High there go forth to them both evil and good. That is to say, for HaShem's portion is His people; they are not under the dominion of princes and the stars of the heavens and their constellations, such that a person could harm them through sorcery and divination like the nations. And this is as Moses said: \"Which HaShem your Elohim has allotted to all the peoples under all the heavens... but you HaShem has taken to be for Him a people of inheritance\" (Deuteronomy 4:19–20), as I explained in the section of Aḥarei Mot (Leviticus 18:25)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The citation to סדר אחרי מות (the section of Aḥarei Mot) with the verse reference Leviticus 18:25 is preserved as Ramban wrote it; Ramban here uses the parashah name to locate his earlier discussion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:17.116671017Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}